\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The first request to surrender a weapon was received as a compliment.\
The Shakti guard unslung his rifle and showed the UDF soldier how neatly it sat against the shoulder, where the balance fell, how little recoil interfered with a sustained expression of displeasure.\
The soldier held out his hand.\
\'93I need you to put it down.\'94\
\'93Why?\'94\
\'93You\'92re being disarmed.\'94\
The guard looked towards the security office.\
Around the forecourt, similar conversations were progressing at different speeds. A few of Alexander\'92s men had already put their weapons down and were beginning to wish they had listened to the whole instruction. Others held their rifles closer. One explained that his pistol was privately owned, as if government soldiers could confiscate an employer\'92s property but would naturally respect his own.\
The Shakti lieutenant came out of the office.\
\'93What the tax are you doing?\'94\
He was instructed to surrender his sidearm.\
\'93We\'92re on the same side!\'94\
The UDF lieutenant\'92s orders contained no provision for that.\
\'93We let you in!\'94\
That part had gone very well.\
The Shakti officer turned back towards the security office. Two soldiers stepped into his path. He brought up his device, selected the perimeter controls and had his wrist caught before he could withdraw the welcome.\
He drove his forehead into the soldier\'92s nose.\
The second soldier grabbed his gun arm. All three hit the doorframe, preventing several men inside from coming to help.\
A guard raised his expensive rifle. The UDF soldier beside him knocked the barrel aside and hit him with the butt of his obsolete one.\
The shot went through the security office window.\
Men who had just congratulated one another on being there began looking for places to be somewhere else. Those who had surrendered their weapons lay down. Those still holding them took cover behind the vehicles they had so helpfully parked.\
The UDF had enough men inside to make its presence difficult to reverse. Alexander\'92s men had enough guns to make occupation unpleasant.\
Along the perimeter, the automated weapons continued watching for unauthorised arrivals.\
The fighting spread from the forecourt into the gardens and between the office buildings. Guards left entrances to reinforce the gate, only to discover that the gate was behind the people they needed to shoot.\
A device rang in the security office.\
\'93Is that Mr Shakti?\'94 someone shouted.\
Nobody could reach it without crossing the broken window.\
